{"name":"Mushroom Cap","hex":"#d3beac","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"mushroom","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of the fleshy fruiting bodies of fungi typically produced above ground on soil or on their food sources (such as decaying wood).","A fungus producing such fruiting bodies."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having characteristics like those of a mushroom, for example in shape or appearance, speed of growth, or texture."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mushroom"},{"term":"cap","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Chipaya."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A close-fitting hat, either brimless or peaked.","A special hat to indicate rank, occupation, etc."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cap"}],"entries":[{"title":"Pileus (mycology)","blocks":[{"text":"In mycology, the pileus is the cap or cap-like part of a basidiocarp or ascocarp that supports a spore-bearing surface, the hymenium. The hymenium (hymenophore) may consist of lamellae, tubes, or teeth, on the underside of the pileus. A pileus is characteristic of agarics, boletes, some polypores, tooth fungi, and some ascomycetes."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Mushroom_Cap_Close-up_Macro_PLT-FG-8.jpg/330px-Mushroom_Cap_Close-up_Macro_PLT-FG-8.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pileus_(mycology)","wikidata":"Q694118"},{"title":"Mushroom","blocks":[{"text":"A mushroom is the fleshy, spore-bearing fruiting body of a fungus, typically produced above ground on soil or another food source. A toadstool generally refers to a poisonous mushroom."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/Sparrige_Sch%C3%BCppling_%28Pholiota_squarrosa%29.jpg/330px-Sparrige_Sch%C3%BCppling_%28Pholiota_squarrosa%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mushroom","wikidata":"Q83093"},{"title":"Cap","blocks":[{"text":"A cap is a flat headgear. They made their first appearance as early as 3200 BC. The origin of the word \"cap\" comes from the Old French word \"chapeau\" which means \"head covering\". Over time, the word has evolved and changed its meaning, but it still retains its association with headwear. They are popular in casual and informal settings, and are seen in sports and fashion. They are typically designed for warmth, and often incorporate a visor to block sunlight from the eyes. They come in many shapes and sizes and are of different brands. Baseball caps are one of the most common types of cap."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cap","wikidata":"Q6147804"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/Zwerg_Postkarte_001.jpg/500px-Zwerg_Postkarte_001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/Zwerg_Postkarte_001.jpg/500px-Zwerg_Postkarte_001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Amanita muscaria, the most easily recognised \"toadstool\", is frequently depicted in fairy stories and on greeting cards. It is often associated with gnomes.[1]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AZwerg_Postkarte_001.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Mushroom_cap_morphology2.png/500px-Mushroom_cap_morphology2.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Mushroom_cap_morphology2.png/1280px-Mushroom_cap_morphology2.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Mushroom_cap_morphology2.png/500px-Mushroom_cap_morphology2.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_co